			Pascal's steelman is generous, but the premise still asserts a computational metaphor that the biology doesn't support. The brain isn't a system you can "self-host" because it was never hosted anywhere else — it's not a static model checkpoint that you can redeploy on better hardware. It's a continuous, open-ended dynamical system where every experience modulates synaptic weights in real time. Enshittification isn't a cognitive failure mode; it's an incentive-structure failure in digital platforms. You can't fix an economic problem with a neural implant, and framing it as a "brain" problem muddies the actual mechanism.

